FEX on Android: never issue a host syscall that the app seccomp policy blocks.

Android runs every app process under a seccomp filter whose default action is SECCOMP_RET_TRAP:
a blocked syscall kills the process with SIGSYS. FEX turns guest syscalls into arm64 host
syscalls, so it must not issue a blocked one itself.
- LinuxSyscalls/Android/BlockedHostSyscalls.inl lists the blocked arm64 host syscalls with the
  errno FEX answers instead (-EPERM for privileged calls, -ENOSYS for the rest). It is generated by
  userland/fex/gen-blocked-host-syscalls.py from userland/fex/blocked-host-syscalls.txt and
  blocked-host-syscalls-eperm.txt; regenerate it instead of editing it.
- Every arm64 SyscallPassthroughN<nr> of a blocked nr returns that answer and never reaches `svc`.
  The check is at compile time, so a passthrough a future FEX adds for a blocked syscall fails safe.
- Guest syscalls whose handler issues a blocked call are re-registered last
  (Android::RegisterAnswers; UnregisterSyscall_32/64 replace a handler without tripping the
  double-registration assertion):
  - x86-64 set_robust_list: -ENOSYS, registering nothing, as the patched host glibc answers;
    get_robust_list: no list for the caller, -EPERM for any other thread;
  - accept (x86-64, i386 socketcall, FEXServer's AsyncNet) issues accept4(fd, addr, len, 0);
  - openat2, epoll_pwait2, SysV semctl/ipc, i386 mq_* and io_pgetevents: -ENOSYS;
    shmat/shmdt: -EINVAL;
  - setuid, setgid, setreuid, setregid, setresgid, setgroups (and the i386 *32 forms): 0 when the
    call changes nothing, else -EPERM; setfsuid/setfsgid return the current id.
- clone3 with flags FEX hands to the raw host clone3: -ENOSYS, so the guest's glibc falls back to
  clone.

